\label{sec:cluster}

Cluster designs move the exposures towards the corners, which shrinks the extrapolation and the bias, and reduce the effective sample to about the number of clusters. At $d=20$ the regression under a cluster design has a lower RMSE than the Bernoulli regression in 68 of 78 graph--mechanism--clustering comparisons and than cluster DM in 62 (79\%), both in 54 (69\%); for the threshold mechanism (d) it beats cluster DM in 2 of 16. Under a Louvain design with about nine clusters on a Barab\'asi--Albert graph, the bias of the regression for (d) falls from 0.68 to 0.24 and its SD rises from 0.18 to 0.34 (Figure~\ref{fig:cluster}, Appendix~\ref{app:extra}). Cluster-bootstrap intervals cover in 83\% of the 396 cluster cells, where HC0 is not valid.

\label{sec:coverage}

The 95\% HC0 interval of the regression covers the true GATE in 73.6\% of the 1800 Bernoulli cells of Figure~\ref{fig:cov}. Coverage is set by the size of the bias relative to the SD and not by leverage: the mean is 93\%, 92\%, 87\%, 67\% and 16\% for $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}$ in $\le0.25$, 0.25--0.5, 0.5--1, 1--2 and $>2$. Among cells with $|{\rm bias}|/{\rm SD}<0.25$ coverage is 94\%, 93\%, 90\% and 95\% by quartile of $L$: leverage costs a few points at most. On all 1800 cells, by graph family (Table~\ref{tab:regime}), coverage is lowest on Stanford3 (0.52) and ego-Facebook (0.66) and highest on Watts--Strogatz (0.82). The bootstrap interval for DM, centred on an estimate biased by $-s\gate$, covers in fewer than 0.1\% of draws at $s=0.5$.
