\paragraph{Flexible learners do not remove the problem.}
On 12--14 cells per mechanism at 200 draws per graph (Figure~\ref{fig:tiers}), none of lasso, ridge, random forest, gradient boosting and a simplified adaptive-threshold H\'ajek estimator has a smaller median RMSE than the regression on mechanisms (a), (b), (c), (e), (f): the five learners' median ratios there are 1.6--3.8. On the threshold mechanism (d), boosting, forests and the adaptive H\'ajek estimator cannot be told apart from it (median ratios 0.99 [0.70, 1.18], 1.09 [0.76, 1.59], 1.02 [0.59, 1.73]; bootstrap over 12--14 cells); lasso and ridge are twice it. Where the mechanism is smooth, a fixed form beats a flexible learner that cannot extrapolate.
